  "strategy": "We are going to use a two-stage optimization approach that combines gradient-based refinement with an adaptive length strategy.\n\nCore idea: Directly optimize the normalized sequence while accounting for clipping effects without relying on LP solvers. This approach leverages analytic gradient calculation from FFT convolutions and injects synthetic signals to explore the solution space more thoroughly.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1. **Initial Population (U-Shaped)**:\n   - Start with a sequence that balances high and low regions (peak intensity gradient profile)\n   - This candidate undergoes an initial gradient descent warm-up for efficiency and stability.\n\n2. **Gradient Descent Optimization**:\n   - For each candidate, compute analytic gradients and perform guided descent\n   - Line searches determine step sizes based on current iteration's signal\n   - Periodically inject artificial signals to disrupt local optima\n\n3. **Length Exploration**:\n   - When signs of convergence occur, evaluate adjacent lengths (n\u2213steps)\n   - Implement a refinement schedule to budget for multiple resolutions\n\n4. **Termination Safeguards**:\n   - Verify all candidates against the evaluation function\n   - Maintain a record of the best verified candidate\n\nDetailed Implementation:\n\n**Step 1: Initialization** (within propose_candidate)\n\n*Algorithm name: U-Shaped Initialization*\n*Implementation:*\n    Choose a sharpness parameter (bracketed between 1 and 5)\n    Pad the frequency domain with a cosine taper at 1% intensity\n    Set gradient descent speed factors optimally for phase transition arrivals\n\n**Step 2:Gradient Descent Mainloop** \n\n*Algorithm name: Frank-Wolfe Adaptive Iterations*\n    Until time budget exhausted:\n        - Perform a complete descent step:\n            gradient = full_derivatives(current_normalized_sequence)\n        - Clip each component to [0,1000] minimum effect\n        - Add injection vectors of diminishing amplitude (archive every 10 steps)\n        - Update most efficient component using line search\n\n**Gradient Calculation using FFT** (np)\n\n    *conv = np.abs(np.fft.rfft(current_normalized_sequence))[:n]\n    *full_conv = compute_full_convolution(current_normalized_sequence)  # using standard indices\n\n**Step 3: Adaptive Signal Geometry**\n\n*Algorithm:* Golden Section Linesearch\n    Sample a wide range of blending ratios\n    Between current optimum and newly perturbed signal\n    Respect updating gradient direction of the optimization field\n\n**Step 4:Length Adjustment Logic** \n\n*Algorithm name: Progressive Resolution Search*\n    Let base length factors:\n        n_current = 800\n        n_try = [n_current + 100, n_current + 200, n_current + 300, n_current + 400, 900]\n        n_try = [t // 10 * 10 for t in sorted unique factors]\n\n    Progressively sample these resolutions until score improvement stalls\n    Include fallback norms from the parent distribution if needed\n\n**Step 5:Terminate and Evaluate**\n\n*Algorithm name: Savitzky-Golay Verification*\n    Use final candidate passed through evaluation function\n    Check for maximum valid score below 1.5027\n    Return one-dimensional sequence as float list\n\nNote:\n\n*Time allocation:* Primary budget to gradient descent, secondary to length exploration.\n\n*Safety net:* If gradient descent indicates unstable progression, revert to parent sequence\n\nFinal assessment: Expected to find candidate with score improvement over 1.506312 while adhering to constraints.",
